# Every decision is recorded in a shared audit room

## Story

As a compliance reviewer opening the record after a call,
I want every moment where the screen chose to answer or chose to stay silent captured in a shared audit room — including what was claimed and what backed it —
so that the whole call is auditable afterward.

## What this is

This is an optional feature that's switched off by default. When it's turned
on, every decision the system makes during a call — whether it lit up the
screen or stayed dark, and why — gets written to a shared room as an audit
record: the thing that was said, the decision, the reason for it, and, for the
answers that did fire, how confident it was and which document backed it.
Opened afterward, the room reads as a complete, in-order account of what the
assistant surfaced during the call and why — the fired answers and the silent
moments alike.

The unbreakable rule: this must never slow the live call down. The entire
promise of the product is answering within a few seconds, so a trip out to the
network can never sit on the answer path. Recording is strictly
fire-and-forget — decisions are dropped into an in-memory holding area and sent
onward in the background. If the room is unreachable, slow, or failing, the
call behaves exactly as if this feature didn't exist. If the holding area
fills up, records are dropped rather than allowed to block the call or pile up
without limit.

## What "done" looks like

- Off by default. With it off, nothing about this feature runs and the rest of
  the system is completely unaffected — it isn't even along for the ride.
- With it on, every decision — fired or silent — is recorded, with its reason,
  and for the fired ones the confidence and the source. Handing a record off
  never waits on the network.
- If the room can't be reached, the call keeps making correct decisions the
  whole time and never blocks or crashes. The feature's own failures are
  quietly swallowed.
- There's an option to leave the customer's actual words out of the room,
  recording only the decision and the reason.
- Turning it on must never measurably slow the call down. That speed guarantee
  outranks how complete or how pretty the room looks — if it can't be met, the
  feature is wrong no matter what.
- None of this can be required for the rest of the system to work, and none of
  the tests may depend on the room actually running.
